48. BM25

BM25 is a classic sparse/lexical retrieval algorithm.

It looks at things such as:

term frequency

inverse document frequency

document length


Suppose query is:

"Java Spring authentication"

BM25 can be excellent at finding documents containing exactly those important terms.


---

49. Why Dense Search Alone Isn't Always Enough

Suppose the user searches:

"error code XJ-92831"

A semantic embedding might understand the general concept of an error.

But exact identifier:

XJ-92831

is extremely important.

Keyword search can be better here.

Similarly:

API-2938
customer ID 78231
SpringBootException

may benefit greatly from lexical matching.


---

50. Hybrid Search

Hybrid search combines:

BM25
+
Vector similarity

Conceptually:

Query
                  |
          +-------+-------+
          |               |
          v               v
       BM25          Vector Search
          |               |
          v               v
       Results          Results
          |               |
          +-------+-------+
                  |
             Rank Fusion
                  |
                  v
             Final Results


---

51. Why Hybrid Search Is Powerful

Imagine the query:

> "How do I fix OAuth XJ-92831 authentication error?"



Dense search understands:

OAuth
authentication
error

BM25 recognizes:

XJ-92831

Together:

semantic understanding
+
exact terminology

can provide stronger retrieval.


---

52. How Do We Combine BM25 and Vector Scores?

One simple conceptual approach is:

\[
Score =
\alpha \cdot SemanticScore
+
(1-\alpha)\cdot BM25Score
\]

where:

\[
0\leq\alpha\leq1
\]

But raw BM25 and vector similarity scores may have different ranges/distributions, so real systems often normalize scores or use rank-based fusion methods such as Reciprocal Rank Fusion.

The important idea:

> Don't blindly add incompatible raw scores.




---

53. IVF

Your image asks:

> IVF vs HNSW



IVF stands for:

> Inverted File Index



The intuition is clustering.

Suppose we have:

1 million vectors

We cluster them:

Cluster 1
Cluster 2
Cluster 3
...
Cluster 1000

Each vector belongs to a cluster.


---

54. IVF Search

Suppose query Q arrives.

First determine:

> Which clusters are closest to Q?



Maybe:

Cluster 14
Cluster 27
Cluster 63

Then search only those clusters.

Instead of:

1,000,000 vectors

we might search:

30,000 vectors

depending on configuration.


---

55. How IVF Is Built

First we train a clustering model, often conceptually like k-means.

Suppose:

vectors
 ↓
clustering
 ↓
centroids

We get:

C1
C2
C3
...
Ck

Each vector is assigned to its nearest centroid.

Then:

C1 → vectors [ ... ]
C2 → vectors [ ... ]
C3 → vectors [ ... ]


---

56. IVF Search

For query:

\[
q
\]

calculate distance to centroids:

\[
d(q,C_1),d(q,C_2),...,d(q,C_k)
\]

Choose the nearest few clusters.

If:

\[
nprobe=5
\]

we might search five clusters rather than all clusters.

Again:

higher nprobe
→ more clusters
→ better recall
→ slower


---

57. IVF vs HNSW

Conceptually:

	IVF	HNSW

Main idea	Clustering	Graph
Navigation	Select clusters	Traverse graph
Search parameter	nprobe	efSearch
Memory	Often relatively efficient	Can be higher
Updates	Can require cluster/index management	Designed for incremental graph insertion
Search	Cluster-based	Graph-based
Main tradeoff	Number of clusters searched	Number of candidates explored


Neither is universally "better."

The right choice depends on:

dataset size

update frequency

latency requirements

recall requirements

memory

filtering

implementation



---

