And there is one particularly important connection to your previous linear-algebra learning

You have been thinking about vectors, dot products, eigenvectors, covariance matrices and geometric transformations. Vector search is a very natural continuation of that journey.

You can view the whole system geometrically:

HIGH-DIMENSIONAL SPACE

             ● document
                     \
                      \
               ●       ●
                    ●
                         ●

       ● ●
      ●
                QUERY
                  ★
                 / \
                /   \
               ●     ●

The embedding model creates the coordinate system.

The embedding places each piece of text at a point in that space.

Cosine/dot product/Euclidean distance defines what it means for two points to be close.

HNSW/IVF creates a navigation structure over those points.

The vector database stores and searches that structure.

And RAG takes the retrieved points/documents and gives them to an LLM.

So the entire subject can ultimately be reduced to one beautiful question:

> How can we represent meaning as geometry, and then search that geometry efficiently?



That is the central idea behind almost everything in your list.
